WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.833
(audience applauding)

00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:01.666
- Good to see you.

00:00:02.928 --> 00:00:04.891
- I mean, keep going.

00:00:04.891 --> 00:00:07.801
(audience applauding)
- Thank you, thank you.

00:00:07.801 --> 00:00:08.634
Very kind.

00:00:08.634 --> 00:00:09.630
- You made it.

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:10.509
- I did make it, yes.

00:00:10.509 --> 00:00:11.342
- We made it.

00:00:11.342 --> 00:00:13.510
- But last time we didn't
quite make it, but yeah.

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:18.420
- And before that I was sick and you came.

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:20.040
I feel like we are passing
ships at this show.

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:21.570
- Ships in the night, yeah.

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:24.546
Just different accidents
happening at different times.

00:00:24.546 --> 00:00:26.250
- Either I have COVID and
you're covering for me

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:27.083
or there's a motor-

00:00:27.083 --> 00:00:27.916
there's always an accident.

00:00:27.916 --> 00:00:28.770
You look great though.

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:29.880
- This is a brand new face.

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:31.200
- Great job, great job.
- It is.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:32.760
It's unbelievable.

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:33.600
What happened?

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:36.690
I was working on a car and I
got a face full of gasoline

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:38.483
and it caught fire.

00:00:38.483 --> 00:00:40.920
I had been eating a flaming hot Dorito

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:42.543
when I bit into it.
- Oh, my god.

00:00:42.543 --> 00:00:43.560
What a commercial.

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:45.360
- Except I set my face on fire.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:46.410
No, but it was...

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:48.750
Yeah, it was interesting.

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:49.800
All third degree burns.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:51.030
It was pretty bad.

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:53.337
It was pretty bad.
- You can't tell at all.

00:00:53.337 --> 00:00:54.810
- No, you think there'd be a zipper here

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:55.920
or something, but no.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:56.790
- That's what I'm saying.

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:57.720
- No, this is like a brand new-

00:00:57.720 --> 00:00:59.580
only for the second time in my career

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:00.980
am I the new face of comedy.

00:01:02.284 --> 00:01:03.510
I got it once in the 80s

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:05.280
and now I get it again.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:06.390
That's a brand new ear.

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:07.620
- Nothing like a great encore.

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:09.630
So not too long after your accident,

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:12.510
you got back into stand-up
pretty quickly, right?

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:13.770
- Yeah, I missed two days.

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:15.360
I spent eight days in the hospital

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:16.860
and they managed to fix this all up.

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:18.120
It was pretty amazing.

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:21.600
This Grossman Burn Center is unbelievable.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:22.433
And I lost-

00:01:22.433 --> 00:01:23.700
it's a great way to lose weight.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:25.860
First you burn off the calories

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:27.930
and then you're going a
crash diet on your motorcycle

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:29.976
and I dropped 20 pounds.

00:01:29.976 --> 00:01:31.200
(Kelly laughs)

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:32.340
It's fantastic.

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:33.173
- The optimist.

00:01:33.173 --> 00:01:36.360
- And of course, the
tabloids get it all wrong.

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:38.490
And the facts that I have
smoother skin, powdery lips,

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:40.815
and a tighter butt, has nothing to do-

00:01:40.815 --> 00:01:41.670
(Kelly laughs)

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:44.521
has nothing to do with
being in the hospital.

00:01:44.521 --> 00:01:46.670
That part was just coincidental

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:47.503
- Natural.

00:01:47.503 --> 00:01:49.440
- Exactly, exactly.

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:50.640
No, but it's good.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:51.473
- I heard that,

00:01:51.473 --> 00:01:54.270
is this true that President
Biden reached out to you?

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:55.103
- He did.

00:01:57.030 --> 00:01:58.680
I've been doing this a long time.

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:00.090
A lot of times you get a call

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:02.010
from the President of United
States and it's an honor

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:03.517
but the way it goes is the phone,

00:02:03.517 --> 00:02:05.280
"Hello? Is this Jay Leno?

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:06.127
Is this the Leno residence?"

00:02:06.127 --> 00:02:06.960
"Yes."

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:08.190
"Are you prepared to take a call

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:09.427
from the President of the United States?"

00:02:09.427 --> 00:02:10.567
"Yes, I am."

00:02:10.567 --> 00:02:12.210
"Please stay on the line,"

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:14.640
and then they'll connect
you with President Obama

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:17.100
or President Bush.
- Yeah.

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:18.877
- So I'm in the hospital, phone rings.

00:02:18.877 --> 00:02:19.710
"Hello?"

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:21.457
"Jay!?"

00:02:21.457 --> 00:02:22.357
"Jay!?"

00:02:22.357 --> 00:02:23.190
"Yeah."

00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:24.357
"It's Joe!"

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:26.887
I said, "Joe?"

00:02:26.887 --> 00:02:27.840
"Joe Biden!"

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:31.290
I went, "Oh, Joe Biden,
President of the United States."

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:32.123
He goes, "Yeah."

00:02:32.123 --> 00:02:32.956
I said, "Where are you?"

00:02:32.956 --> 00:02:34.537
"I'm in China."

00:02:34.537 --> 00:02:36.090
"I heard about... I just wanna send..."

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:37.350
I said, "Oh, I'm doing fine.

00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:38.317
I'm very honored you call."

00:02:38.317 --> 00:02:39.630
"Yeah, no problem."

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:40.685
Just hilarious.

00:02:40.685 --> 00:02:42.333
- Just straight up called you yourself.

00:02:42.333 --> 00:02:43.710
- Just straight to "Jay, it's Joe."

00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:45.750
Of course, of course.

00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:49.410
- Literally, he and his
wife called my mother.

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:50.940
'Cause we did this thing
and it was about teachers

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:52.260
and they called my mother.

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:53.790
Nobody else called.

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:54.630
Like you said.

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:56.880
Not a representative for them.

00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:59.340
Literally my mom was like, "Hello?"

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:00.630
And it was them.

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:03.210
And it was the craziest thing I've seen.

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:04.470
Definitely like a man of the people.

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:05.303
- He really is.

00:03:05.303 --> 00:03:06.270
I like Joe very much.

00:03:06.270 --> 00:03:07.230
A very nice guy.

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:09.150
- So, wait, you must have
met a lot of presidents

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:10.002
over your time.

00:03:10.002 --> 00:03:11.460
Did you meet Reagan?

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:12.293
Did I hear that?

00:03:12.293 --> 00:03:13.710
- Yeah, when I was just
starting my career,

00:03:13.710 --> 00:03:15.330
I got to the White House Correspondent.

00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:17.130
That was the scariest one.

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:19.350
The first time I did the White
House Correspondents' Dinner.

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:22.440
Like I'm backstage and
it's all political people

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:24.300
and some show people.

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:26.010
And I'm standing backstage in the wings,

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:27.930
and this general comes in like this.

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:28.920
He's got all the-

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:30.480
He goes, "Hey, you the comedian?

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:31.828
Come here, come here.

00:03:31.828 --> 00:03:34.320
Okay, that's the President
of the United States.

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:35.153
You understand that?

00:03:35.153 --> 00:03:36.330
That is my commander-in-chief."

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:37.163
He's doing this.

00:03:37.163 --> 00:03:37.996
"That's my commander."

00:03:37.996 --> 00:03:38.970
I go, "yes."
- He's doing that to you?

00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:40.680
- Yeah, he goes, "You don't denigrate him.

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:41.700
You don't make fun of him.

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:43.640
He is the President of the United States.

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:44.760
Do you understand what I'm telling you?"

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:46.860
I said, "Yes, yes sir."

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:48.090
He's a big huffer, fine.

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:50.940
He leaves and I go, "Oh man,
I gotta change my act here."

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:51.833
You know?

00:03:51.833 --> 00:03:53.100
(audience laughs)

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:55.186
And then about two minutes later,

00:03:55.186 --> 00:03:57.780
remember George Shultz,
Secretary of Defense?

00:03:57.780 --> 00:03:59.970
George Schultz comes, he's got a drink.

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:02.550
And he's like three sheets
to the wind at this point.

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:04.597
He goes, "Leno, come here, come here."

00:04:04.597 --> 00:04:08.261
"You get out there, you nail
Ronnie's ass to the wall."

00:04:08.261 --> 00:04:09.840
(Kelly laughs)

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:10.673
- It's totally different.

00:04:10.673 --> 00:04:12.127
- I go, "Well, that General-"

00:04:12.127 --> 00:04:14.977
"Screw him, he works for me."

00:04:14.977 --> 00:04:15.967
"I'm telling you what to do."

00:04:15.967 --> 00:04:18.060
"You make fun of that black
thing on Reagan's head,

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:18.927
you understand?"

00:04:20.377 --> 00:04:23.400
"You understand, I want you
to nail him to the wall."

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:25.246
I don't know what to do now.

00:04:25.246 --> 00:04:26.079
- [Kelly] What did you do?

00:04:26.079 --> 00:04:28.239
- Oh, I nailed him to the wall.

00:04:28.239 --> 00:04:30.750
(Kelly &amp; audience applauding)

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:31.740
- You had an out.

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:33.220
They told you to.

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:35.710
- Yeah, I remember the opening joke was

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:38.940
I wanna congratulate Nancy Reagan

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:41.790
on being named Humanitarian of the Year.

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:42.840
I'm glad she beat out

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:44.910
that conniving little tramp Mother Teresa.

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:45.810
That was the joke.

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:48.257
But Reagan goes (imitates loud laughing).

00:04:49.387 --> 00:04:52.680
When Reagan laughed at
that, then I knew I was in.

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:53.520
- And they're all watching.

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:54.353
Does he think it's funny?

00:04:54.353 --> 00:04:56.250
Yes, it's hilarious.

00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:57.540
- When you do the wife,
that's what they do.

00:04:57.540 --> 00:04:58.620
They look at the President.

00:04:58.620 --> 00:04:59.790
Oh, and then they laugh.

00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:01.340
That's exactly what happened.

00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:04.677
- So tell us about the new
season of "You Bet Your life."

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:09.030
- You know, it's a lot of fun.

00:05:09.030 --> 00:05:11.310
You get the oddest people,

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:13.260
'cause it's really a
variation of jaywalking,

00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:14.970
what we used do on "The Tonight Show."

00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:17.820
- It's a game show with a comedy...

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:20.940
Or a comedy show with a game
attached to it, you know?

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:22.350
But we just get strange.

00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:24.630
Like, you have a mathematician
on here today, right?

00:05:24.630 --> 00:05:25.470
- Yes.

00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:27.450
- I just met her, fascinating woman.

00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:29.130
And we had this guy,

00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:31.290
this guy says, "I'm in Mensa."

00:05:31.290 --> 00:05:34.080
He says, "My friends are always amazed

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:35.190
when we go out to dinner.

00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:38.280
I can know 50% of their bill like that."

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:39.727
I said, "Oh, you do that quickly?"

00:05:39.727 --> 00:05:41.850
"Yes, I'm like a human computer."

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:44.160
I said, "Okay, what's 15% of 500?"

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:47.280
And he goes, "Okay, 10% of 500 is 50."

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:48.980
I go, "Okay, hold it right there."

00:05:50.490 --> 00:05:52.563
I'm dyslexic, that's what I do.

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:55.560
I said, "You should have had the answer

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:57.780
before I finish the question."

00:05:57.780 --> 00:05:58.860
It just made me laugh.

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:01.920
Just odd people.

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:03.720
- They don't even realize
what's coming outta their mouth.

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:04.680
- No, we had another woman

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:07.020
who was a professional pooper-scooper.

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:08.127
That's what she did.
- What?

00:06:08.127 --> 00:06:10.983
- But she was a professional,
not your average.

00:06:10.983 --> 00:06:12.261
(audience laughs)

00:06:12.261 --> 00:06:13.710
She worked in New York City

00:06:13.710 --> 00:06:15.870
and she would follow people
around with their dogs.

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:18.150
But she was a professional pooper-scooper.

00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:20.627
I said, well, that must
be a fascinating job.

00:06:20.627 --> 00:06:24.000
Just odd, different kind
of people like that.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:25.950
- I didn't even know that was a job.

00:06:25.950 --> 00:06:28.323
I thought it was your
job if you owned the dog.

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:30.810
- I don't really think it's a job.

00:06:30.810 --> 00:06:31.743
- Okay, okay.

00:06:33.301 --> 00:06:36.060
- Is it true that when
you first came to L.A.,

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:38.610
you would sleep in open houses?

00:06:38.610 --> 00:06:40.320
- Well, you can't do that anymore because-

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:43.053
- I don't think you were
supposed to do it before.

00:06:44.310 --> 00:06:46.980
- Well, every house has
an alarm system now.

00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:48.660
But when I first came to L.A.,

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:50.400
first thing I did was buy a car.

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:52.440
It's either a car or a place to live.

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:53.820
Well, you need a car, you're in L.A.

00:06:53.820 --> 00:06:55.710
So I would just sleep in my car.

00:06:55.710 --> 00:06:57.210
And when I would go to open houses,

00:06:57.210 --> 00:06:59.760
that are open Sunday from noon to four.

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:01.080
So I'd get there at 3:30.

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:02.040
Look around the house.

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:04.290
Okay, "Thank you, realtor, thank you."

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:06.510
I'd slam the front door, then
I'd go hide in the closet.

00:07:06.510 --> 00:07:08.370
I'd stand there and
then by half hour later,

00:07:08.370 --> 00:07:09.750
I'd hear the realtor lock up.

00:07:09.750 --> 00:07:11.310
And I would live in the
house for three or four days.

00:07:11.310 --> 00:07:12.467
- That's crazy.

00:07:12.467 --> 00:07:14.610
- And I never broke anything.

00:07:14.610 --> 00:07:16.140
I always made the bed.

00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:17.820
And I did that a bunch of times.

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:19.140
And then I was staying
at a beautiful house

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:20.910
on Coldwater Canyon
where I later found out

00:07:20.910 --> 00:07:22.980
was owned by one of the Beach Boys.

00:07:22.980 --> 00:07:25.297
So I'm sleeping, I hear the realtor,

00:07:25.297 --> 00:07:27.351
"Now in here, here's a lovely room."

00:07:27.351 --> 00:07:30.000
(imitates child screaming)

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:31.147
This little nine-year-old girl goes,

00:07:31.147 --> 00:07:32.497
"Mommy, there's a man in the bed."

00:07:32.497 --> 00:07:33.330
"Oh, my God!"

00:07:33.330 --> 00:07:34.380
And I go, "Oh, sorry."

00:07:34.380 --> 00:07:35.576
Realtor goes, "What are you doing here?"

00:07:35.576 --> 00:07:36.727
Realtor starts screaming.

00:07:36.727 --> 00:07:37.717
"Sorry, excuse me."

00:07:37.717 --> 00:07:39.796
"Let me get outta here, please."

00:07:39.796 --> 00:07:40.629
- [Kelly] Oh, my god.

00:07:40.629 --> 00:07:41.550
- But actually it was a great place-

00:07:41.550 --> 00:07:42.960
You saved a fortune in rent

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:43.794
and you lived in-

00:07:43.794 --> 00:07:44.627
(audience &amp; Kelly laugh)

00:07:44.627 --> 00:07:45.833
and I lived in beautiful homes.

00:07:45.833 --> 00:07:47.760
- I think it's called squatting.

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:49.010
- Yeah, no, it was great.

00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:50.760
Yeah, you could say that.

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:52.408
- Yeah, I think you do say that.

00:07:52.408 --> 00:07:54.690
(laughs)

00:07:54.690 --> 00:07:57.030
I had to live in my car for
a few days when I lived here

00:07:57.030 --> 00:07:58.440
because my place burned down.

00:07:58.440 --> 00:07:59.580
That's uncomfortable.

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:00.413
- It is, it is.

00:08:00.413 --> 00:08:02.520
- Yeah, even in the recline position.

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:05.869
- But see I had a '55
Buick, which is enormous.

00:08:05.869 --> 00:08:06.750
- Oh.
- Oh yeah.

00:08:06.750 --> 00:08:08.850
You could seat six for dinner in this car.

00:08:08.850 --> 00:08:10.620
- Okay, you could lay out.
- Yeah.

00:08:10.620 --> 00:08:11.610
In fact, it didn't have a radio.

00:08:11.610 --> 00:08:12.480
It had live acts.

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:14.240
That's how big it was.

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:15.118
- Live bands.

00:08:15.118 --> 00:08:15.951
- Live bands, yeah.

00:08:15.951 --> 00:08:17.963
It was a big, it was a big car.

